WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.710
Disney's live-action Beauty And The Beast
is a gorgeous reimagining of the beloved 1991

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:09.840
animated classic and it's loaded with callbacks
and easter eggs to the animated movie that

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:11.770
many even casual viewers will notice.

00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:17.310
But the new movie also includes many delicious
details that only super-fans might spot the

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:18.359
first time around!

00:00:18.359 --> 00:00:22.750
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and in this video, I'm going to reveal 17

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:28.099
easter eggs and details that only real fans
will notice in the new Beauty And The Beast.

00:00:28.099 --> 00:00:32.140
And by the way, I'll be including some fascinating
facts from my interviews with Beauty And The

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:36.560
Beast director Bill Condon and composer Alan
Menken about how they used deleted scenes,

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:40.020
characters, lyrics and dialogue from the original
animation in the new movie.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:44.969
Just before I start, I'm giving away this
amazing Disney hardback book from DK, which

00:00:44.969 --> 00:00:49.309
is loaded with behind-the-scenes artwork,
photos, and information about Disney's live-action

00:00:49.309 --> 00:00:51.079
and animated movies over the years.

00:00:51.079 --> 00:00:55.100
For a chance to win, all you have to do is
subscribe and leave a comment about the new

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:58.700
Beauty And The Beast movie or your favourite
easter egg from the film.

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:02.829
And for bonus ways to enter, check out the
Gleam link in the video description below.

00:01:02.829 --> 00:01:07.310
Ok, quick warning: there are spoilers ahead,
so if you've not seen the movie yet, why not

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:11.730
check out my spoiler-free review, then come
back here after you've seen the film!

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:15.700
When Beast suggests Belle use the enchanted
map to 'travel to the one place [she's] always

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:20.091
wanted to see', the destination she chooses
is a lovely nod to a Beauty And The Beast

00:01:20.091 --> 00:01:22.420
cameo in another Disney animated movie!

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:23.820
Can you guess where I'm going with this?

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:28.220
Yes, Belle and Beast travel to Paris, where
Beast suggests they visit Notre Dame, all

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:32.590
of which is very exciting because Belle popped
up in Disney's animated feature The Hunchback

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:36.670
of Notre Dame, where, in typical Belle style,
she walked along the street while reading

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:37.670
a book.

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:42.520
There's an interesting moment during the opening
song "Belle" where Belle enquires if Monsieur

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:46.299
Jean has lost something again, and he replies
that he believes he has, but he can't remember

00:01:46.299 --> 00:01:47.299
what.

00:01:47.299 --> 00:01:50.829
Given that Emma Watson's best known role before
Beauty And The Beast was as Hermione Grainger

00:01:50.829 --> 00:01:54.601
in the Harry Potter series, this feels like
a homage to the scene in the first film, The

00:01:54.601 --> 00:01:58.869
Philosopher's Stone, where after Neville gets
his Remembrall, he says to Hermione that he

00:01:58.869 --> 00:02:00.640
can't remember what he'd forgotten.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:04.540
Although Harry Potter fans may well love this
line, Beauty And The Beast director Bill Condon

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:08.790
has said that it's a happy coincidence because
the reason Monsieur Jean can't remember what

00:02:08.790 --> 00:02:12.950
he's forgotten is because of the Enchantress's
spell and how it's made the townspeople forget

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:15.530
that the castle and the enchanted objects
ever existed.

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:20.640
So, what Jean can't remember here is that
he's actually Mr Potts, and his conversation

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:24.910
with Belle is some clever foreshadowing for
the reveal later on that he's married to Mrs

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:25.910
Potts.

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:30.040
Perhaps a somewhat unfortunate consequence
of the changed dialogue is that we never got

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:33.870
that brilliant line from the original where
the baker rudely interrupts Belle with the

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:34.870
line, "Marie!

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:35.870
The baguettes!"

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:38.790
- because he's bored of listening to her talk
about the book she's been reading.

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:42.590
In the new movie, Belle's conversation with
Monsieur Jean replaces that conversation with

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:46.250
the baker, but [perhaps] there's a nod with
a twist to that moment as Belle still gets

00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:49.750
brushed off, but this time it's by Monsieur
Jean when he says the book she's returning

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:51.780
to Pere Robert sounds boring.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:56.150
His line is also a joke at the expense of
the story of Romeo and Juliet which is the

00:02:56.150 --> 00:03:00.170
book Belle's just been reading in this film
and also the one she taught the Beast to read

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:02.020
in the original animation.

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:06.250
In the new movie, the Beast also pokes fun
at Romeo and Juliet when he introduces Belle

00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:10.250
to his library saying there are so many things
that are better to read than that Shakespearian

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:12.940
tragedy about heartache and pining.

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:17.840
Jean Cocteau's 1946 film La Belle et la Bête
is one of director Bill Condon's favourite

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:22.319
movies and he found it a source of inspiration
for his own 21st-century remake of the Beauty

00:03:22.319 --> 00:03:23.319
And The Beast story.

00:03:23.319 --> 00:03:27.010
For instance, there's a different set-up to
the 1991 movie for Belle's father.

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:31.520
In this version, Maurice is taken prisoner
by Beast for picking a white rose.

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:35.840
Given Condon's love for the French film, his
use of the white rose is a hat-tip to Cocteau's

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:40.250
film where Belle's father is caught by the
Beast when he takes a rose from Beast's garden,

00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:45.510
the idea for which itself came from the original
mid-18th-century French story.

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:49.400
And Beauty And The Beast's filmmakers included
a nice easter egg and homage to the author

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:54.110
of that very first Beauty And The Beast story,
Gabrielle-Suzanne Barbot de Villeneuve, when

00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:56.950
they named Belle's hometown Villeneuve.

00:03:56.950 --> 00:04:02.530
Shortly after Maurice enters the Beast's castle,
seeking shelter, Cadenza the harpsichord starts

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:07.420
playing 'Be Our Guest', but suddenly stops
when Maurice goes into the room.

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:11.319
This is a delightful easter egg to the fact
that, in the animated movie, 'Be Our Guest'

00:04:11.319 --> 00:04:15.769
was initially actually going to be performed
by the enchanted objects for Maurice, rather

00:04:15.769 --> 00:04:17.049
than for Belle.

00:04:17.049 --> 00:04:21.400
Maestro Cadenza, who's married to the Italian
opera singer Madame de Garderobe and becomes

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:26.430
a harpsichord under the curse, is a new character
created for the live-action adaptation.

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:31.310
But Cadenza's inclusion in this film is still
a hat-tip to Villeneuve's original story as

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:36.259
well as to LePrince de Beaumont's adaptation
a few years later, which feature Beauty playing

00:04:36.259 --> 00:04:40.970
the harpsichord in her own home, and also
reveal that Beast has a harpsichord in his

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:41.970
castle.

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:46.930
On top of that, Maestro Cadenza is also a
little musical homage to the 1997 direct-to-video

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:50.750
midquel, Beauty And The Beast: The Enchanted
Christmas, in which the main villain was the

00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:55.930
music conductor and composer Maestro Forte,
who became a pipe organ during the curse.

00:04:55.930 --> 00:05:00.340
The live-action film makes this connection
when, just before 'Be Our Guest', Cogsworth

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:05.479
asks Cadenza to 'play quietly', and Candeza
replies, 'Sotto voce, of course.'

00:05:05.479 --> 00:05:10.180
Sotto voce and Forte are musical terms which
are basically opposites of each other, which

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:14.680
is fitting because Cadenza is a good character
and Forte was villain.

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:18.820
The songs in the new Beauty And The Beast
also include homages to a variety of classic

00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:19.820
musicals.

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:23.520
For "Be Our Guest", director Bill Condon has
confirmed the new musical number includes

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:28.610
over a dozen references including West Side
Story, Singin' In The Rain, Cabaret, and Chicago,

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:32.740
which Condon wrote the screenplay for, as
well as nods to Esther Williams and Busby

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:35.680
Berkeley, both of which also influenced the
1991 version.

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:40.200
Plus, there's also a little reference to Moulin
Rouge, which starred Ewan McGregor, who plays

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:42.729
Lumiere in Beauty And The Beast.

00:05:42.729 --> 00:05:46.500
When Disney were going through the music archives
of the animated film, they came across some

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:50.669
original lyrics by the late Howard Ashman
which didn't make it into the final film back

00:05:50.669 --> 00:05:51.669
in 1991.

00:05:51.669 --> 00:05:56.440
So, director Bill Condon decided to put them
to good use in his live-action version.

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:58.199
And when I interviewed Bill Condon recently,
I asked him what those new lyrics were, and

00:05:58.199 --> 00:06:02.150
he told me that they're the last lines that
Emma Thompson sings as Mrs Potts when she

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:06.690
reprises the Beauty And The Beast song at
the end of the movie, which are: "Winter turns

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:13.360
to spring, famine turns to feast, nature points
the way, nothing left to say, Beauty and the

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:14.360
Beast."

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:15.420
But that's not all, folks!

00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:19.699
When I interviewed Alan Menken, he told me
that some of Howard Ashman's unused lyrics

00:06:19.699 --> 00:06:23.770
from the 1991 movie also made it into the
live-action version of the song Gaston!

00:06:23.770 --> 00:06:28.530
The lyrics in question begin with Gaston singing:
'When I hunt, I sneak up with my quiver, and

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:35.300
beasts of the field say a prayer; first, I
carefully aim for the liver, then I shoot

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:38.160
from behind.'

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:42.130
By the way, Menken told me that those lyrics
didn't make it into the original film as 'sneaking

00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:46.970
up and shooting an animal in the liver was
probably a bit much for a younger audience'.

00:06:46.970 --> 00:06:50.570
Music boxes play an important part in the
new film as Belle's father, Maurice, is an

00:06:50.570 --> 00:06:51.900
artist who makes them.

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:54.420
But they're also a call-back to the animated
feature.

00:06:54.420 --> 00:06:59.930
In early drafts of the 1991 script, Mrs Potts'
son Chip only had one sentence of dialogue,

00:06:59.930 --> 00:07:03.770
and the other scenes where he appears in the
film as we know it today were going to feature

00:07:03.770 --> 00:07:09.199
in his place a Music Box character which spoke
only by making little chiming noises.

00:07:09.199 --> 00:07:12.960
But when the producers realised the movie
would benefit from a child's perspective,

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:17.050
they increased Chip's role and got rid of
the music box character.

00:07:17.050 --> 00:07:21.620
After Chip says his famous line from the original
animation, 'Momma, there's a girl in the castle',

00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:25.860
he follows it up by asking Mrs Potts what
kind of tea the girl likes and then lists

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:30.310
a few different types including chamomile,
which is a delicious easter egg to the original

00:07:30.310 --> 00:07:34.430
name the filmmakers were going to give the
talking teapot in their animated movie.

00:07:34.430 --> 00:07:40.340
Yes, back in 1991, Mrs Potts was very nearly
named Mrs Chamomile, after the soothing herbal

00:07:40.340 --> 00:07:44.380
tea, but the filmmakers decided to go with
the name we know and love today as they thought

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:47.160
'chamomile' might be difficult for young children
to say.

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:51.699
Toward to the start of the movie, we see Belle
teaching a young girl to read and pointing

00:07:51.699 --> 00:07:53.460
to a page with a Blue Bird on it.

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:58.530
The page says "The Blue Bird that flies over
the dark wood", which is a nod to the blue-birds

00:07:58.530 --> 00:08:02.639
that fly over the wood at the start of the
animated movie, and also to the blue-coloured

00:08:02.639 --> 00:08:04.910
bird that appears during the song "Something
There".

00:08:04.910 --> 00:08:09.000
When we hear that song in the original film,
Belle is trying to bring out the Beast's gentler

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:11.789
side by encouraging him to feed the birds
in his garden.

00:08:11.789 --> 00:08:15.289
At first, they're all scared away with the
exception of a little blue bird who jumps

00:08:15.289 --> 00:08:16.699
into his hand.

00:08:16.699 --> 00:08:21.170
By the way, look closely at what Belle's wearing
as she teaches the young girl and you'll see

00:08:21.170 --> 00:08:25.260
that her cardigan has blue birds embroidered
into the pattern.

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:30.120
After Gaston and LeFou finish their big song-and-dance
number at the tavern, Gaston compliments his

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:34.959
loyal side-kick on how great he is and asks,
'How is it that no girl has snatched you up

00:08:34.959 --> 00:08:39.219
yet?', and LeFou replies with a nice easter
egg to Frozen, saying that, 'I've been told

00:08:39.219 --> 00:08:41.479
I'm clingy, but I really don't get it.'

00:08:41.479 --> 00:08:46.400
Josh Gad, of course, plays both LeFou and
Frozen's Olaf, an adorable snowman who just

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:50.951
loves introducing himself to the movie's human
characters with the line, 'Hi, I'm Olaf, and

00:08:50.951 --> 00:08:55.029
I like warm hugs', and who's also happy to
melt for the right person.

00:08:55.029 --> 00:08:59.400
As well as being a Frozen easter egg, LeFou's
reply also foreshadows what the movie's director

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:03.160
has called the character's "gay moment" at
the end of the movie.

00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:06.899
Just outside the tavern's doors, there are
two wooden carvings of wild boar heads, and

00:09:06.899 --> 00:09:10.930
one is used by the angry mob to try to ram
down the door to the Beast's castle.

00:09:10.930 --> 00:09:14.660
Although this is a change from the animated
film, where the villagers chop down a tree

00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:19.410
to batter the Beast's door, it's actually
a really nice hint to the design of the Beast.

00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:23.230
Wild boars were one of the animals that Disney
artist Glen Keane took inspiration from when

00:09:23.230 --> 00:09:27.310
he was creating the animated Beast's look
– in fact, if you look at the Beast in the

00:09:27.310 --> 00:09:31.300
1991 movie, you'll see he has the tusks of
a wild boar.

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:35.860
But long before Disney's much-loved classic,
the Beast was already associated with boars.

00:09:35.860 --> 00:09:39.760
Back in the late nineteenth century, for example,
the English artist Walter Crane portrayed

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:43.920
the Beast as a wild boar in his illustrations
for children's picture books.

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:48.290
Speaking of boars, the coat of arms in the
Beast's castle consists of a lion, a boar,

00:09:48.290 --> 00:09:49.339
and letters WD.

00:09:49.339 --> 00:09:54.149
Just as boars influenced the Beast's design
in the animated movie, so too did lions – with

00:09:54.149 --> 00:09:56.120
the Beast having a lion's mane.

00:09:56.120 --> 00:10:00.399
As for the WD monogram, that's a nod to Walt
Disney!

00:10:00.399 --> 00:10:04.070
When Belle is surprised that Beast is able
to complete a quote she reads from Shakespeare's

00:10:04.070 --> 00:10:08.339
A Midsummer Night's Dream, he explains that
he had 'an expensive education.'

00:10:08.339 --> 00:10:12.130
This is a change from the animation where
the Beast has trouble reading, but it's also

00:10:12.130 --> 00:10:16.290
a reference to the role that made actor Dan
Stevens, who plays the Beast, famous.

00:10:16.290 --> 00:10:21.080
In the popular period drama Downton Abbey,
Stevens's character Matthew Crawley also enjoyed

00:10:21.080 --> 00:10:25.930
an expensive education, which included a private
boys-only boarding-school followed by Oxford

00:10:25.930 --> 00:10:27.070
University.

00:10:27.070 --> 00:10:30.700
And interestingly enough, that's mirrored
in Stevens' own life as he too attended a

00:10:30.700 --> 00:10:35.140
private boarding school before going on to
study at Cambridge University.

00:10:35.140 --> 00:10:38.510
At the end of this movie, Belle looks at the
Prince and asks, 'How would you feel about

00:10:38.510 --> 00:10:42.890
growing a beard?', which director Bill Condon
told me is an unused line of dialogue that

00:10:42.890 --> 00:10:46.770
lyricist Howard Ashman came up with for the
animated classic.

00:10:46.770 --> 00:10:50.380
Interestingly enough, Glen Keane, who designed
the animated Beast for that film, didn't want

00:10:50.380 --> 00:10:54.670
the Beast to turn back into the Prince at
the end of the movie so had Paige O'Hara who

00:10:54.670 --> 00:10:59.720
played Belle record that line about the beard,
though, of course, they didn't end up using

00:10:59.720 --> 00:11:00.720
it back then.

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:04.140
Now, I'd love to hear if you spotted any other
easter eggs or interesting references in the

00:11:04.140 --> 00:11:05.450
new Beauty And The Beast movie.

00:11:05.450 --> 00:11:09.149
And how do you think it compared to the original
animated classic?

00:11:09.149 --> 00:11:13.290
Don't forget to comment and subscribe for
your chance to win this awesome hardback Disney

00:11:13.290 --> 00:11:14.290
book!

